# Convex Backend Rules

## Schema (`convex/schema.ts`)

- Define ALL tables in a single `schema.ts` file — this is the single source of truth.
- Always use strict schema validation with `v` validators.
- Add indexes for any field you query or filter by.

```typescript
import { defineSchema, defineTable } from "convex/server";
import { v } from "convex/values";

export default defineSchema({
  users: defineTable({
    name: v.string(),
    email: v.string(),
    avatarUrl: v.optional(v.string()),
  }).index("by_email", ["email"]),

  messages: defineTable({
    conversationId: v.id("conversations"),
    authorId: v.id("users"),
    body: v.string(),
  }).index("by_conversation", ["conversationId"]),
});
```

## File Organization

```
convex/
  tables/               # Table definitions (one file per table)
    index.ts            # Re-exports all tables
    profiles.ts         # profiles table definition
  lib/                  # Shared internal helpers (DB lookups, auth, validation)
    auth.ts             # e.g. getAuthenticatedUserId
    profiles.ts         # e.g. getNicknameOwner, getProfileByUserId
  auth/                 # Auth domain — exported queries/mutations only
    profiles.ts
    users.ts
  game/                 # Game domain — exported queries/mutations only
    players.ts
    sessions.ts
  schema.ts             # Imports from tables/ and composes the schema
```

- **`convex/tables/`** — one file per table definition, re-exported via `index.ts`. `schema.ts` imports and composes them.
- **`convex/<domain>/`** — only exported Convex functions (`query`, `mutation`, `action`).
- **`convex/lib/`** — only shared internal helpers (plain async functions, not Convex functions). Domain files import from `lib/`, never define private helpers inline.
- One file per resource/entity within each domain folder.
- NEVER edit anything in `convex/_generated/`.

## Queries

- Use `query()` for reading data. Always validate args with `v` validators.
- Use `.withIndex()` instead of `.filter()` for indexed fields.
- Use `.collect()` only when result set is small (< 100 docs). Use `.take(n)` or pagination for large sets.
- Always await promises — never fire-and-forget.

```typescript
import { query } from "./_generated/server";
import { v } from "convex/values";

export const list = query({
  args: { conversationId: v.id("conversations") },
  handler: async (ctx, args) => {
    return await ctx.db
      .query("messages")
      .withIndex("by_conversation", (q) => q.eq("conversationId", args.conversationId))
      .order("desc")
      .take(50);
  },
});
```

## Mutations

- Use `mutation()` for writes. Always validate args.
- Keep mutations small and focused — one logical write operation.
- Return the ID of created documents when applicable.

```typescript
import { mutation } from "./_generated/server";
import { v } from "convex/values";

export const send = mutation({
  args: {
    conversationId: v.id("conversations"),
    authorId: v.id("users"),
    body: v.string(),
  },
  handler: async (ctx, args) => {
    return await ctx.db.insert("messages", {
      conversationId: args.conversationId,
      authorId: args.authorId,
      body: args.body,
    });
  },
});
```

## Actions

- Use `action()` only for side effects (external APIs, file storage).
- Actions cannot read/write the database directly — call mutations/queries via `ctx.runMutation` / `ctx.runQuery`.

## Naming Convention

- Export names should be verb-based: `list`, `get`, `send`, `create`, `update`, `remove`.
- File names match the table/resource they operate on.
- Access functions via `api.messages.send`, `api.users.get`, etc.
